 "steps": [
  {
   "id": "s0",
   "title": "A piston that floats freely",
   "text": "The gas is under a **freely moving piston**, so the pressure outside decides the pressure inside and it stays **constant**. We will change the temperature. The gas now is at {{T|0}} K.",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "T": 300
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "s1",
   "title": "Heat the gas",
   "text": "Heat the cylinder. The molecules speed up (they turn redder). They hit the piston harder, push it out, and the gas **expands** until the pressure is balanced again.",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "T": 300
     },
     "at": 0
    },
    {
     "tween": {
      "T": 500
     },
     "at": 0,
     "dur": 4200
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "s2",
   "title": "V ∝ T on the graph",
   "text": "The red dot moves along a **straight line that passes through the origin**: the volume is proportional to the temperature in **kelvin**. At {{T|0}} K the volume is {{L/4|2}} V₀ (V₀ is the volume at 300 K).",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "T": 500
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "s3",
   "title": "Cool the gas",
   "text": "Cool it down: molecules slow (blue) and the piston sinks. At 150 K the volume has fallen to half of what it was at 300 K.",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "T": 500
     },
     "at": 0
    },
    {
     "tween": {
      "T": 150
     },
     "at": 0,
     "dur": 4800
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "s4",
   "title": "Why kelvin, not °C",
   "text": "The line points to **zero volume at 0 K**, which is −273 °C. Doubling the **Celsius** temperature does not double V, but doubling the **kelvin** temperature does. Always use \\( T = t + 273 \\).",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "T": 300
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "readouts": [
  {
   "label": "Temperature (K)",
   "value": "{{T|0}}",
