  }
 ],
 "formulas": [
  {
   "title": "Charles's law",
   "tex": "\\role{th}{\\dfrac{V_1}{T_1}} = \\role{th}{\\dfrac{V_2}{T_2}}",
   "text": "At constant pressure, V/T stays the same. T must be in kelvin."
  },
  {
   "title": "Celsius to kelvin",
   "tex": "T\\,(\\mathrm{K}) = t\\,(^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}) + 273",
   "text": "Absolute zero: 0 K = −273 °C."
  }
 ],
 "challenges": [
  {
   "id": "c1",
   "type": "number",
   "title": "Now you try",
   "prompt": "A gas occupies **3 L** at **300 K**. It is heated to **600 K** at constant pressure. What is the new volume in litres?",
   "answer": 6,
   "hint": "Use \\( V_1/T_1 = V_2/T_2 \\) with kelvin.",
   "explain": "\\( 3/300 = V_2/600 \\), so \\( V_2 = 6 \\) L. Doubling the kelvin temperature doubles the volume.",
   "tolerance": 0.1,
   "unit": "L"
  },
  {
   "id": "c2",
   "type": "number",
   "title": "Now you try",
   "prompt": "A gas at **27 °C** has volume **2 L**. At constant pressure it is heated until its volume is **4 L**. What is the new temperature in kelvin?",
   "answer": 600,
   "hint": "First convert 27 °C to kelvin.",
   "explain": "27 °C = 300 K. The volume doubles, so the kelvin temperature doubles: **600 K**.",
   "tolerance": 1,
   "unit": "K"
  },
  {
   "id": "c3",
   "type": "choice",
   "title": "Now you try",
   "prompt": "What happens to the volume of a gas at constant pressure if its kelvin temperature is **halved**?",
   "options": [
    {
     "id": "a",
     "label": "It doubles"
    },
    {
     "id": "b",
     "label": "It stays the same"
    },
    {
     "id": "c",
     "label": "It is halved"
    },
    {
     "id": "d",
     "label": "It becomes four times larger"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "c",
   "hint": "V is proportional to T.",
   "explain": "V ∝ T, so half the temperature gives half the volume."
  }
 ],
 "takeaways": [
  "**Charles's law:** at constant pressure, \\( V/T = \\text{constant} \\) (T in kelvin).",
  "The V–T graph is a **straight line through the origin**.",
  "Hotter molecules push the piston out until the pressure is balanced: **heating at constant pressure expands the gas**."
 ],
 "notes": [
  {
   "title": "Connects to",
   "text": "This is the **isobaric process** (constant pressure). The gas expands and does work \\( W = P\\Delta V \\) — see *Work done by a gas*. The heat it takes in is more than the energy stored: see the **first law**."
  }
 ]
}